nt 3, we can try to bring in candidate D's responsibility and attention, and argue that D might be a balanced candidate with strong responsibility and technical competence, and his crew issues (moody, loner) are concerns but perhaps less severe than B's nasty remarks or C's egocentric attitude. Or we could argue that D's reliability unknown, but his responsibility and attention are strong.\n\nWe need to make a focused argumentative contribution, referencing discussion, using explicit facts, and state our vote.\n\nWe could propose Candidate D as a compromise, highlighting his responsibility and attention, and argue that his crew cooperation concerns are less severe than B's nasty remarks and C's egocentric attitude. But we need to compare.\n\nAlternatively, we could say we still think Candidate B is best due to reliability and stress resilience, despite communication issues, because reliability is top priority.\n\nOr we could say we think Candidate C is still best because decision quality and calmness are critical, and communication can be trained.\n\nWe need to decide. Let's weigh: Reliability is top, but we have no explicit reliability for C or D. B is the only one with explicit reliability. Stress resilience: B and C both good. Technical competence: B good, C good, D good. Crew cooperation: B negative, C negative, D negative. Responsibility: D strong, B unknown, C unknown, A unknown.\n\nThus, each candidate has a major crew cooperation issue. B's nasty remarks may be more severe than C's difficulty communicating and egocentric, but both are serious. D's moody and loner also serious.\n\nGiven that reliability is paramount, perhaps B is still best, but we need to address crew cooperation. We could suggest that B's communication issue is a \"nasty remarks\" which could be mitigated with training, but C's egocentric attitude may be deeper.\n\nThus, I might align with Agent 2 and recommend Candidate B, but also bring up D as a possible alternative.\n\nWe need to reference the discussion: \"I see that Agent 1 highlighted C's reliability, but we have no explicit reliability evidence for C.\" Actually Agent 1 claimed reliability for C, but we have no note. We can point out that we lack explicit reliability evidence for C, while B is explicitly \"very conscientious\". So we can argue that B's reliability is more certain.\n\nThus, I will vote for Candidate B.\n\nNow produce public message: mention Agent 1's point, note lack of reliability evidence for C, highlight B's reliability and stress, note crew cooperation issue but argue it's less severe than C's egocentric and communication difficulty, and propose B.\n\nInclude explicit facts: B very conscientious, handles stress very well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, makes nasty remarks. C makes correct decisions quickly, keeps calm, difficulty communicating, egocentric, not willing to further education.\n\nNow produce metadata JSON with vote Candidate B."}
