er skills, nasty remarks, weak memory for numbers. Also we have no explicit evidence of responsibility for B. For C we have: makes correct decisions quickly, keeps calm, understands complicated technology, difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education, puts concern for others above everything, excellent attention skills. Also we have \"Candidate C is known to be 100% reliable\" from initial facts. So we have reliability for C as well. So both have reliability evidence. B's reliability is \"very conscientious\". C's is \"100% reliable\". Both strong. B's weak memory for numbers is a concern for attention. C's communication difficulty and egocentric are concerns for crew cooperation. Which is more severe? Both are safety-critical. We need to argue. As Agent 2, we can either maintain B or shift to C. We need to decide. In earlier turn, Agent 2 argued for B, citing reliability and stress resilience, and said communication issue may be remediable. Now Agent 1 counters that C's reliability is explicit and attention is excellent, and B's memory weakness is more concerning. Agent 2 can respond: maybe still B because reliability and stress are top, and memory weakness can be mitigated, while C's egocentric attitude may be deeper. Also note that C's lack of willingness to further education is a concern for adaptability. So we can maintain B. Provide reasoning: B's reliability and stress resilience are critical, and his technical competence is strong; memory weakness can be mitigated; communication issue is serious but can be addressed; C's egocentric attitude and lack of adaptability may be harder to change. So we keep vote for B.\n\nThus produce public message: \"I understand Agent 1's points, but I still recommend B because...\" Provide evidence. Then metadata vote B.\n\nProceed."}
