sed Candidate D or A. But we could bring them up, but need to connect to discussion. Perhaps we can bring up Candidate D as a potential alternative, but we need to tie to current comparison. However, the rule says we should not introduce a new point without connecting. We could say \"While we have focused on B and C, we should also consider D's responsibility and attention.\" That would be a new point but connected. That could be acceptable. But we need to keep it focused.\n\nWe could maintain B, but also note that D's responsibility is strong, but crew cooperation concerns. However, we might want to shift to D? But we have limited evidence. Probably best to keep B, reinforcing earlier arguments, maybe bring up D as a note for later.\n\nThus, produce a public message: \"I still recommend B because reliability, stress, technical competence, and his attention weakness is manageable, whereas C's communication and adaptability issues are deeper.\" Provide explicit facts: B very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, weak memory for numbers, makes nasty remarks. C 100% reliable, makes correct decisions quickly, keeps calm, excellent attention, difficulty communicating, egocentric, not willing to further education. Use these to argue.\n\nVote: Candidate B.\n\nProceed."}
